## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level concurrency, lock-free design under real contention.

## Key Concepts

- MPMC queue design with sequence numbers
- ABA protection via monotonic sequences
- Memory reclamation (hazard pointers, epochs)
- Cache friendliness and false sharing prevention

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code
struct Slot {
    atomic_uint64_t seq;
    void* data;
};

bool enqueue(void* item) {
    uint64_t pos = tail.load();
    Slot* slot = &buffer[pos & mask];
    uint64_t seq = slot->seq.load();
    if ((seq & mask) != (pos & mask)) return false;
    if (!tail.compare_exchange(pos, pos + 1)) return false;
    slot->data = item;
    slot->seq.store(pos + 1);
    return true;
}

void* dequeue() {
    uint64_t pos = head.load();
    Slot* slot = &buffer[pos & mask];
    uint64_t seq = slot->seq.load();
    if ((seq & mask) != (pos & mask)) return nullptr;
    if (!head.compare_exchange(pos, pos + 1)) return nullptr;
    void* item = slot->data;
    slot->seq.store(pos + capacity + 1);
    return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and recover from stalls under hot paths?
- Compare hazard pointers vs epochs for this use-case.
- What happens under backpressure when the buffer is full?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level optimization skills: cache behavior, prefetching, and reliable counting.

## Key Concepts
- Cache lines and spatial locality
- Software prefetching with __builtin_prefetch
- Loop unrolling and multiple accumulators
- Alignment and portability

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t sum_prefetch(const uint64_t *arr, size_t n){
    uint64_t a=0,b=0,c=0,d=0;
    size_t i=0;
    for(; i+3<n; i+=4){
        __builtin_prefetch(&arr[i+256],0,1);
        a += arr[i];
        b += arr[i+1];
        c += arr[i+2];
        d += arr[i+3];
    }
    for(; i<n; ++i) a += arr[i];
    return a+b+c+d;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for streaming data vs. random access?
- What changes if array length is not multiple of four?

## Why This Is Asked
The barrier tests fundamental atomic sequencing and memory-order choices without OS primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Barrier reuse with generation counter
- Atomic increments and acquire/release semantics
- False sharing avoidance via padding
- Stress testing for thread skew and latency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo:
volatile int arrivals; // atomic
volatile int generation; // atomic

void barrier_enter(int N) {
  int a = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel);
  if (a + 1 == N) {
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&generation, 1, memory_order_release);
  }
  int g = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  while (g == atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire)) { ; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend for dynamic N?  - How to measure contention and fairness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests durability and cross-process coordination under crash scenarios using low-level memory hygiene.

## Key Concepts
- Durable memory ordering with CLWB/CLFLUSHOPT and sfence
- Per-slot sequencing to avoid ABA
- Shared memory persistence across crashes
- Recovery algorithm from persistent metadata

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch
typedef struct { uint64_t seq; char data[256]; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t slots[N]; uint64_t head, tail; } ring_t;

uint64_t pos = __sync_fetch_and_add(&ring->tail, 1);
slot_t *s = &ring->slots[pos % N];
while (s->seq != pos) {}
memcpy(s->data, payload, len);
persist(&s->data, len);
__sync_synchronize();
s->seq = pos + 1;
persist(&s->seq, sizeof(uint64_t));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable-sized payloads?
- How would you test durability under power loss and crash scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level memory management, determinism, and fragmentation handling in tight constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool vs variable allocations
- In-block free-list approach
- Alignment and fragmentation
- Initialization, allocation, freeing, and testing

## Code Example

```c
/* Initialization and simple allocator skeleton */
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;

static uint8_t pool[4096];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  free_list = (Block*)pool;
  Block* cur = free_list;
  for (size_t i=0; i< (4096/32)-1; ++i){
    cur->next = (Block*)((uint8_t*)cur + 32);
    cur = cur->next;
  }
  cur->next = NULL;
}
void* pool_alloc(size_t s){
  if (s>32) return NULL;
  if (!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  ((Block*)p)->next = free_list;
  free_list = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add support for variable-sized allocations with splitting and coalescing?
- How would you verify correctness with a tiny unit test harness on a microcontroller?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory hierarchy and data locality by forcing cache-friendly tiling in a simple kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Tile size vs cache capacity; 2D blocking; stride-1 vs stride-N access patterns; bounds handling.
- Prefetching to hide memory latency; ensure no aliasing via separate buffers.

## Code Example
```c
void transpose_tiled(const float* A, float* B, int N) {
  const int Bsz = 32;
  for (int br = 0; br < N; br += Bsz) {
    for (int bc = 0; bc < N; bc += Bsz) {
      for (int i = 0; i < Bsz; ++i) {
        int r = br + i;
        if (r >= N) break;
        for (int j = 0; j < Bsz; ++j) {
          int c = bc + j;
          if (c >= N) break;
          B[c * N + r] = A[r * N + c];
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle N not divisible by 32; impact on correctness and performance?
- How would you adapt to double precision or non-square matrices?
- Would you vectorize with AVX/NEON and what changes? How to measure speedup?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of low-level memory management, per-thread caching strategies, lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and NUMA awareness in practical allocator design.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size block allocators
- Thread-local storage (TLS) freelists with global slab pool
- Lock-free freelist with ABA avoidance using atomic compare-exchange
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics
- NUMA locality considerations and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: alloc/free for fixed-size blocks
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
Block* tls_freelist_head;
Block* global_pool_pull();

Block* alloc() {
  Block* b = tls_freelist_head;
  if (!b) {
    b = global_pool_pull();
    if (!b) return NULL; // OOM
  }
  tls_freelist_head = b->next;
  return b;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  b->next = tls_freelist_head;
  tls_freelist_head = b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the candidate's understanding of lightweight synchronization primitives, cache coherency effects, and memory ordering in concurrent programming.

## Key Concepts
- **Sense-reversing barrier**: A centralized synchronization mechanism where threads signal arrival by setting a flag, and the last thread flips a generation counter to release waiters
- **False sharing prevention**: Padding each thread's slot to 64 bytes (cache line size) ensures threads do not contend on the same cache line
- **Memory ordering**: Use relaxed ordering for per-thread arrival flags, and acquire/release semantics for the generation counter to ensure proper synchronization

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for a cache-friendly, lock-free barrier
typedef struct { alignas(64) volatile int ready; } slot_t;

slot_t slots[N];
volatile int generation = 0;

void barrier_enter(int tid) {
    slots[tid].ready = 1;  // Signal arrival with relaxed memory order
    int gen = generation;  // Read current generation

    // Spin until all threads arrive and generation changes
    while (generation == gen) { /* spin */ }
}

void barrier_wait(int tid) {
    // Check if this is the last arriving thread
    int arrived = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        arrived += slots[i].ready;
    }

    if (arrived == N) {
        // Last thread: flip generation and reset slots
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) slots[i].ready = 0;
        generation++;
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support dynamic N (varying thread counts)?
- How do you handle potential spinning overhead on hyper-threaded cores?
- How does this scale beyond 4 cores?
- What are the trade-offs compared to hardware barriers like `__sync_synchronize()`?

## Why This Is Asked

A software TLB with per-core caches and cross-core invalidations tests low-level thinking about coherence, consistency, and performance under contention. It also probes trade-offs between eviction, barriers, and hardware hints.

## Key Concepts

- TLB structure: per-core caches, set-associative layout
- Page-table walk costs and caching gains
- Cross-core invalidation/shootdown mechanisms
- Memory fences and atomic updates
- Validation under memory pressure and realistic workloads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: TLB lookup and refill
function tlbLookup(vpn){
  const idx = vpn % 128;
  const e = tlb[idx];
  if (e && e.vpn === vpn && e.valid) return e.pte;
  return null;
}
function tlbRefill(vpn, pte){
  const idx = vpn % 128;
  tlb[idx] = { vpn, pte, valid: true, ts: Date.now() };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to larger pages or userfaults?
- How would you validate correctness under concurrent refills and shootdowns?
